// ---- ARC: ISABELLA'S BACKSTORY AND REDEMPTION (3 nodes) ----
TPN_NODES['tpn_isabella_origins'] = {text:`Isabella was not always Mama.\n\nYou find her file in the depths of Minerva's shelter — a personnel dossier stamped with the Grace Field seal, labeled CARETAKER CANDIDATE 73584. The photograph is a girl, maybe twelve, with dark hair and hollow eyes. The same face. Younger. Unbroken.\n\nThe file reads like a horror story told in clinical language.\n\nSubject 73584, born at Plant 3, Grace Field complex. Scored consistently in the top 0.1 percentile. Identified as premium harvest candidate at age six. Scheduled for shipment at age twelve.\n\nShipment deferred. Subject demonstrated exceptional caretaking aptitude and emotional intelligence. Reclassified: Mama candidate.\n\nConditions of reclassification: Subject must bear one biological child via artificial insemination. The child will be placed in a farm house. Subject will not be told which house. Subject will raise cattle children knowing her own child is among them — or not — with no way to confirm.\n\nSubject accepted.\n\nYou stare at the page. Isabella chose to become the farmer because the alternative was becoming the meat. She was twelve years old. She was given a choice that was not a choice, by a system designed to ensure compliance through the most intimate cruelty imaginable — making a mother raise children she would eventually send to slaughter, never knowing if one of them was her own.\n\nRay is quiet for a long time.\n\n"She is my mother," he says. Not a question. He has known for years — pieced it together from blood types and birthdates and the way Isabella's lullaby matched a melody he remembered from before memory should begin. "She hummed it when I was an infant. The song Leslie wrote. She recognized me. She always knew."\n\nEmma's eyes are wet. "She raised us knowing what we were for. She fed us, taught us, loved us — and she was going to let them eat us."\n\n"She loved us AND she was going to let them eat us," Norman corrects quietly. "Both things are true. That is what the system does. It makes love into a weapon."`,scene:'revelation',mood:'#636e72',characters:['Isabella','Ray','Emma','Norman'],effects:{stats:{int:1,per:1},relationships:{isabella:2,ray:1}},choices:[{text:'This changes how you see her — she was a victim too. Plan to save her along with the farm children.',next:'tpn_isabella_love',effects:{stats:{cha:1},relationships:{isabella:3}}},{text:'Understanding her past does not excuse her choices. She had decades to rebel and chose comfort instead.',next:'tpn_isabella_love',effects:{stats:{int:1},relationships:{ray:1}}},{text:'Show this file to the others — everyone deserves to know what the system does to its caretakers.',next:'tpn_isabella_love',effects:{stats:{cha:1,per:1}}}]};
TPN_NODES['tpn_isabella_love'] = {text:`The question haunts you as you plan the final assault on the farm system: what do you do with the Mamas?\n\nThere are dozens of them — one for every farm house across the demon world. Women who were once cattle children themselves, who survived by becoming the very thing that nearly destroyed them. Each one a jailer. Each one a victim. Each one loving the children she raises with a tenderness that is real and monstrous in equal measure.\n\nRay speaks first, and his voice is stripped of its usual cynicism. "She broke the tracker disabling device I built. She reported my escape preparations to headquarters. She played me for six years — letting me think I was the spy when she knew the entire time." He pauses. "And every night, she sang me Leslie's song. The song her dead friend wrote before they shipped him. She sang it to me — her son — knowing I would never know why it mattered."\n\nEmma shakes her head. "The system is the enemy. Not the people it consumed. Isabella did not build the farms. She survived them. Badly, yes. Cruelly, yes. But she was a child who was told: become a monster or become meat."\n\n"Some would have chosen meat," Norman says.\n\n"And they are dead," Ray replies. "The brave ones are all dead. The survivors are the ones who compromised. That is what makes the system perfect — it filters for compliance and calls it strength."\n\nYou think about Isabella's face at the wall. The crack in her mask. The way she said go. Be free. Be what I could not be.\n\nShe could have stopped you. She had the authority, the trackers, the power of the entire demon establishment behind her. She let you go. In the end, after twenty years of compliance, she chose the children over the system.\n\nOnce.\n\nIs once enough?\n\nEmma thinks so. Emma always thinks so. That is her gift and her blindness — she believes in people the way others believe in gravity. Automatically. Without evidence.\n\nBut this time, you think she might be right.`,scene:'confrontation',mood:'#636e72',characters:['Isabella','Ray','Emma','Norman'],effects:{stats:{cha:1},relationships:{isabella:2,ray:1,emma:1}},choices:[{text:'Include the Mamas in the liberation plan — free them alongside the children they raised.',next:'tpn_isabella_sacrifice',effects:{stats:{cha:2},relationships:{isabella:2}}},{text:'Focus on the children first — the Mamas made their choices and can make new ones after.',next:'tpn_isabella_sacrifice',effects:{stats:{per:1}}}]};
TPN_NODES['tpn_isabella_sacrifice'] = {text:`Isabella dies on the steps of Grace Field House.\n\nIt happens during the final liberation — when every farm in the demon world opens simultaneously, when thousands of children stumble blinking into a freedom they do not understand. Peter Ratri's loyalists make a last stand, and the demon soldiers under their command converge on the original Grace Field, where it all began.\n\nIsabella is there. Not as Mama. Not as a caretaker. As a mother.\n\nShe stands between the demon soldiers and the children — her children, all of them, every single one she raised and fed and sang to sleep and measured for slaughter. She has no weapons. She has no plan. She has twenty years of intimately understanding exactly how the system's enforcers operate, because she was one of them.\n\n"You will not touch them," she says.\n\nThe demon commander hesitates. Isabella was a model Mama — the highest-rated caretaker in the system. Her farm produced the finest meat. Her compliance was legendary. Seeing her defect is like watching a load-bearing wall crack.\n\n"Stand aside, Isabella. You are valuable. We can reassign you."\n\n"I was reassigned once. A frightened twelve-year-old girl was told she could die or she could become this. I chose wrong. I chose wrong for twenty years." Her voice does not shake. "I am choosing right now."\n\nThe soldiers advance. Isabella does not move. The children behind her are screaming, crying, begging her to run.\n\nRay reaches her first. He grabs her arm. "Mother—"\n\nShe looks at him. Really looks. The mask is gone. There is nothing left but the girl who loved a boy named Leslie, who hummed his song to a baby she was never supposed to acknowledge, who spent two decades in a prison of her own compliance.\n\n"You were always the reason," she whispers. "Every song. Every night. You were the reason I did not lose myself completely."\n\nShe pushes him behind her. The soldiers fire.\n\nIsabella falls.\n\nThe children escape. Every single one. Because a woman who spent her whole life as a prison guard used her last breath to hold the door open.\n\nRay carries her body out of Grace Field. He does not speak for three days. When he finally does, he says only one thing:\n\n"Her name was Isabella. She was my mother. And in the end, she was free."`,scene:'battle',mood:'#cc0000',characters:['Isabella','Ray','Emma'],effects:{stats:{cou:1},relationships:{isabella:3,ray:2},flags:{isabella_sacrificed:true}},choices:[{text:'Honor her sacrifice — carry the liberation forward to the human world.',next:'tpn_human_world_arrival',effects:{stats:{cha:1},relationships:{ray:1}}},{text:'Let grief fuel the final push — every remaining farm falls today.',next:'tpn_peter_ratri',effects:{stats:{cou:2,str:1}}}]};
// ---- ARC: GOLDY POND BATTLE (3 nodes) ----
TPN_NODES['tpn_goldy_pond_arrival'] = {text:`The coordinates from Minerva's shelter lead you somewhere wrong. Somewhere designed.\n\nGoldy Pond looks beautiful from the distance — a settlement nestled beside a crystalline lake, surrounded by architecture that blends human and demon styles. Stone buildings. Gardens. A clocktower. It looks like a town. It looks like safety.\n\nIt is a hunting ground.\n\nYou realize it too late. The gates close behind your scouting party — you, Emma, and three others who volunteered for the advance team. The rest of the group is still outside, hidden in the forest with Norman and Ray.\n\nA voice echoes across the settlement, amplified by speakers mounted on every building. It speaks in the demon tongue, but a human translator crackles through a second later:\n\n"Welcome to Goldy Pond. Hunting Ground of Lord Bayon and the Aristocratic Sport Hunters. The hunt begins at dawn. You have twelve hours to prepare. Weapons are cached throughout the settlement. We encourage you to arm yourselves — the sport is better when the prey fights back."\n\nGoldy Pond is a private preserve where demon aristocrats hunt human children for entertainment. Not for food. For fun. The children are released into the settlement, given a head start, and then pursued by demons who treat the slaughter as a weekend hobby.\n\nBut something is wrong with the script. The settlement is not empty.\n\nA hand grabs your arm and pulls you into a building. A girl — maybe fourteen, covered in scars, carrying a weapon that looks hand-forged from scavenged metal — presses a finger to her lips.\n\n"Quiet. They have cameras. My name is Violet. I have been here for two years. And you are not going to die today — because we have been waiting for reinforcements."\n\nShe leads you down a hidden stairway into a bunker beneath the clocktower. Inside: children. A dozen of them. Armed. Scarred. Eyes that have seen too much death and decided to stop accepting it.\n\n"Welcome to the Goldy Pond Resistance," Violet says. "We have a plan to kill every aristocrat who hunts here. We just needed enough fighters to execute it."`,scene:'revelation',mood:'#cc0000',characters:['Emma'],effects:{stats:{per:1,cou:1}},choices:[{text:'Join the resistance immediately — these children have been fighting alone long enough.',next:'tpn_goldy_pond_resistance',effects:{stats:{cou:1},relationships:{emma:1}}},{text:'Gather intelligence first — you need to understand the hunting ground before committing to a battle plan.',next:'tpn_goldy_pond_resistance',effects:{stats:{int:1,per:1}}}]};
TPN_NODES['tpn_goldy_pond_resistance'] = {text:`The Goldy Pond resistance has been fighting for three years. Not escaping — fighting. They tried to escape. The walls are impenetrable. The surrounding forest is patrolled by Lord Bayon's personal guard. The only way out is through.\n\nTheir leader is a boy named Oliver — sixteen, missing his left hand (taken by a demon hunter as a trophy), with a tactical mind that rivals Norman's. He spreads a hand-drawn map of Goldy Pond across a makeshift table.\n\n"Five aristocratic demons hunt here. They rotate — two per hunt, cycling through a schedule that brings all five together twice a year. The next convergence is in three days. For the first time in three years, all five will be in the settlement simultaneously."\n\nEmma leans forward. "You want to kill all five at once."\n\n"We have been building toward this. Stockpiling weapons. Studying their patterns. Each aristocrat has a weak point — a regeneration core in their body. Destroy the core, and they cannot heal. They die like anything else."\n\nHe marks each demon's hunting pattern on the map. Lord Bayon — the host, a strategic hunter who drives prey into traps. Lady Nous — she hunts in bonded pairs with her partner, Nouma. Leuvis — the most dangerous, an ancient demon who fights for the thrill and has never been injured by a human. And two younger nobles who hunt recklessly and rely on overwhelming strength.\n\nThe plan is intricate. Ambush points. Kill zones. Fallback positions. Every resistance fighter has a role. Every contingency has been mapped.\n\n"The problem," Oliver says quietly, "is Leuvis. He is older than the farm system. Stronger than any demon we have encountered. Faster. Smarter. And he loves to fight. He WANTS us to resist — it makes the hunt better for him."\n\nEmma studies the map. Her expression is the one you have learned to recognize — the one that means she is about to volunteer for the most dangerous part of the plan.\n\n"I will take Leuvis," she says.\n\nOliver stares at her. "He has killed over a hundred humans in this arena. Trained fighters. Adults."\n\nEmma's smile is sharp enough to cut. "He has never fought someone from Grace Field."`,scene:'investigation',mood:'#636e72',characters:['Emma'],effects:{stats:{int:1},relationships:{emma:1}},choices:[{text:'Support Emma against Leuvis — she cannot face the ancient demon alone.',next:'tpn_goldy_pond_battle',effects:{stats:{cou:2},relationships:{emma:2}}},{text:'Take a different sector — trust Emma with Leuvis while you eliminate the other aristocrats.',next:'tpn_goldy_pond_battle',effects:{stats:{str:1,per:1}}}]};
TPN_NODES['tpn_goldy_pond_battle'] = {text:`Dawn of the convergence hunt. Five aristocratic demons enter Goldy Pond expecting sport. They find war.\n\nThe resistance strikes simultaneously across five positions. Oliver's three years of preparation detonate in a single coordinated assault that turns the hunting ground into a kill zone — but with the roles reversed. The prey are hunting the predators.\n\nLord Bayon falls first. He walks into an ambush so layered that even his centuries of experience cannot untangle it in time. Three resistance fighters hit his regeneration core with forged-metal spears while a fourth collapses a building onto his escape route. He dies confused. He dies afraid. For the first time in his immortal life, he understands what his prey felt.\n\nNous and Nouma fight as a bonded pair, covering each other's blind spots with telepathic coordination. Oliver's team separates them using a flashbang improvised from chemicals found in the settlement's supply caches. Alone, Nouma panics. Nous, trying to reach her partner, exposes her core. They fall within minutes of each other.\n\nThe younger nobles die fighting. They are strong and fast but arrogant — they have never imagined humans as a genuine threat. That failure of imagination kills them.\n\nLeuvis is different.\n\nEmma faces him in the clocktower square. The ancient demon is enormous — three meters tall, six-armed, his mask carved with the faces of every human he has killed. He moves like smoke. His strikes crack stone.\n\nEmma moves like water. Grace Field trained her body without her consent — the daily tests, the games of tag, the physical drills disguised as play. Every measurement that rated her as premium meat also made her the fastest, most agile human in the demon world.\n\nShe cannot overpower Leuvis. She does not try. She baits him into a corridor rigged with explosives. She uses his love of combat against him — letting him chase, letting him think he is winning, letting his hunter's pride pull him exactly where she needs him.\n\nThe explosion takes his right side. His regeneration kicks in — but slower now, damaged. You hit his exposed core with a metal spike driven by every ounce of strength you have left.\n\nLeuvis falls. He looks up at Emma with his one remaining eye. And he laughs.\n\n"Finally," he whispers. "A worthy hunt."\n\nHe dies smiling. The monster dies smiling.\n\nGoldy Pond is free. The walls are breached. The surviving children — resistance fighters and fresh arrivals alike — pour into the demon forest. Scarred, bleeding, victorious.\n\nOliver clasps Emma's hand with his remaining one. "Grace Field," he says. "I should have known. You farm kids are something else."\n\nEmma does not answer. She is staring at the clocktower, at the scorch marks and the blood, at the arena where children were hunted for sport.\n\n"We are tearing it all down," she says. "Every farm. Every hunting ground. Every system that treats children as things. All of it."`,scene:'battle',mood:'#cc0000',characters:['Emma'],effects:{stats:{str:2,cou:2}},choices:[{text:'Rally the Goldy Pond survivors and return to the main group — your army just grew.',next:'tpn_minerva_shelter',effects:{stats:{cha:1}}},{text:'Search Goldy Pond for intelligence — the aristocrats must have records, maps, communication devices.',next:'tpn_peter_ratri',effects:{stats:{int:2}}}]};
// ---- ARC: MUJIKA AND SONJU'S TRUE NATURE (2 nodes) ----
TPN_NODES['tpn_mujika_truth'] = {text:`Mujika tells you the truth on a night when the forest is quiet enough to hear your own heartbeat.\n\nYou have been traveling with the two heretic demons for eleven days. They have fed you, protected you, taught you to survive. Mujika braids flower crowns for the younger children. Sonju teaches the older ones how to track and forage. They are gentle. They are kind.\n\nThey are not what they seem.\n\n"I am what the demon aristocracy calls the Evil-Blooded," Mujika says, staring into the campfire. The flames paint her face in shifting shadows. "My lineage carries a mutation — a bloodline trait that allows demons to maintain their form and intelligence without consuming human flesh. Any demon who ingests my blood gains this ability permanently. It is hereditary. It spreads."\n\nShe holds up her hand. In the firelight, you can see her veins — dark, almost black, pulsing with something that is not quite blood.\n\n"The demon royal family has hunted my kind for centuries. Not because we are dangerous. Because we are unnecessary. If every demon carried the Evil Blood, no demon would need human meat. The farms would serve no purpose. The entire aristocratic power structure — which depends on controlling the food supply — would collapse overnight."\n\n"They killed my family. Every Evil-Blooded demon they could find. I am the last. Sonju protects me because he believes in a world where demons choose what they are, rather than being enslaved by hunger."\n\nSonju speaks from the shadows. His voice is careful. Measured. "I should tell you my own truth, since we are being honest." He steps into the firelight. "I do not eat human flesh. But I want to. The craving is constant. Mujika's blood could free me from it — but I refuse. I choose to resist the hunger every day as an act of will. If I am freed by blood rather than choice, the resistance means nothing."\n\nHe pauses. "I also believe the Promise should be renegotiated, not destroyed. If demons and humans share a world again — without the farms, without the walls — humans will be wild. Free. And some demons, freed from the addiction, will still choose to hunt. Not from need. From nature."\n\nEmma stiffens. "You mean you want to hunt humans. Free-range instead of farmed."\n\nSonju does not deny it. "I want the CHOICE to exist. I want a world where the hunt is honest — predator and prey, both free, both aware. Not this obscenity of cages and breeding and measured skulls."\n\nThe campfire crackles. The silence is heavy.\n\nMujika places her hand over Sonju's. "He will not act on it. I know him."\n\n"You know what I am today," Sonju replies. "None of us know what we will be tomorrow."\n\nYou look at Emma. Her face is unreadable. She is processing — not just the revelation about Sonju, but the larger question it raises: can you ally with someone whose vision of the future includes your species as prey? Can you trust a friend who admits, openly and honestly, that their nature runs counter to your survival?\n\nThe demon world is not simple. The monsters are not simple. Nothing, you are learning, is ever simple.`,scene:'revelation',mood:'#636e72',characters:['Mujika','Sonju','Emma'],effects:{stats:{int:1,per:1},relationships:{mujika:2,sonju:-1}},choices:[{text:'Accept the complexity — Mujika and Sonju are allies despite the contradictions. Trust is not the same as agreement.',next:'tpn_mujika_blood_choice',effects:{stats:{cha:1},relationships:{mujika:2,sonju:2}}},{text:'Confront Sonju directly — you need to know if he will be a threat when the Promise falls.',next:'tpn_mujika_blood_choice',effects:{stats:{cou:1},relationships:{sonju:1}}}]};
TPN_NODES['tpn_mujika_blood_choice'] = {text:`The question of Mujika's blood becomes the most important strategic decision of the resistance.\n\nNorman wants to use it as a weapon. "We synthesize her blood. Mass-produce it. Distribute it through demon water supplies, food chains, trade routes. Within a generation, every demon is Evil-Blooded. The hunger disappears. The farms become pointless. The system collapses without a single battle."\n\nHis plan is elegant. Ruthless. Effective. It is also, as Ray points out, functionally genocide.\n\n"You are talking about forcibly altering an entire species' biology without their consent," Ray says. "That is what the farms do to us — modify us, breed us, shape us for someone else's purposes. You want to do the same thing to them."\n\nNorman's expression does not change. "The alternative is war. Children will die in a war. My way, no one dies."\n\n"No one dies, but an entire civilization is fundamentally altered against its will. Demons who WANT Mujika's blood should receive it. Demons who do not—"\n\n"Will continue eating children."\n\nThe argument splits the group. Emma, predictably, sides with neither. She wants a third path — one that does not require force-feeding an entire species or leaving the farm system intact while negotiations drag on.\n\nMujika listens to all of it. Then she raises her hand, and the room goes quiet.\n\n"My blood is mine," she says. "Not a weapon. Not a tool. Mine. I will share it with any demon who asks. I will not allow it to be forced on anyone." She looks at Norman. "You are brilliant, child. But you are also a boy who was raised in a cage and told his worth was measured by the weight of his brain. Do not build new cages and call them freedom."\n\nNorman flinches. It is the first time you have seen anyone land a blow on his composure since Lambda.\n\nEmma takes Mujika's hand. "We do it your way. Consent. Choice. We offer the blood to every demon who wants to be free of the hunger. And for the ones who refuse — we find another way."\n\n"There may not be another way," Norman says.\n\n"Then we make one," Emma replies. "That is what we do. We make ways where there are none."\n\nThe plan shifts. Instead of biological warfare, you build a distribution network — demons who want Mujika's blood can seek it out. Mujika becomes not a weapon but a beacon. A proof of concept that demons can exist without human flesh.\n\nIt is slower than Norman's plan. It is messier. It requires trusting demons to make the right choice.\n\nBut it is not a cage. And after Grace Field, you have had enough of cages.`,scene:'confrontation',mood:'#636e72',characters:['Mujika','Norman','Emma','Ray'],effects:{stats:{cha:1,int:1},relationships:{mujika:3,norman:1}},choices:[{text:'Help build the blood distribution network — travel with Mujika to demon settlements willing to listen.',next:'tpn_seven_walls_labyrinth',effects:{stats:{cha:1},relationships:{mujika:2}}},{text:'While Mujika spreads the blood, focus on finding the Seven Walls — the Promise itself must change.',next:'tpn_seven_walls_labyrinth',effects:{stats:{int:1,cou:1}}}]};
// ---- ARC: THE CHILDREN'S GROWING LEADERSHIP (3 nodes) ----
TPN_NODES['tpn_children_lessons'] = {text:`Teaching children to survive in a demon world was never part of any plan. But the plan stopped mattering three days into the forest, when you realized that thirty-eight children cannot be carried by four teenagers forever.\n\nSo you teach them.\n\nEmma teaches movement. How to run silently on leaf litter. How to climb a tree in under ten seconds. How to fall without breaking bones. She turns it into games — because she is Emma, and because the truth is too heavy for six-year-olds. "Silent tag" becomes stealth training. "The floor is lava" becomes obstacle navigation. "Hide and seek" becomes camouflage technique.\n\nThe children learn terrifyingly fast. They were bred for intelligence, after all. The farm system's greatest cruelty is also its greatest miscalculation — it raised children smart enough to dismantle it.\n\nRay teaches reading. Not storybooks — maps, field guides, the survival manuals from Minerva's shelter. He sits with clusters of five-year-olds and teaches them to identify edible plants, read compass headings, interpret demon-script warning signs. His teaching style is blunt and unsentimental. The children adore him anyway.\n\nNorman teaches strategy. War games with pebbles on dirt maps. "If the demon comes from here, what do you do?" Seven-year-old Chris answers: "Regroup signal three, fallback to position B, youngest children evacuate first." Norman nods. Chris beams.\n\nBut it is the weight of it that gets to you. Watching an eight-year-old girl practice knife grips with the focused intensity of a soldier. Listening to a six-year-old recite evacuation protocols before bed, the way other children might recite prayers. Seeing Phil — quiet, brilliant Phil — organize the younger children into buddy pairs with the calm authority of someone three times his age.\n\nThese children should be learning arithmetic and finger-painting. Instead, they are learning to survive. The gap between what should be and what is stretches wider every day, and the tension lives in your chest like a second heartbeat.\n\nEmma feels it too. You catch her one night, after the children are asleep, sitting at the edge of camp with her head in her hands.\n\n"I am teaching them to fight," she whispers. "I am teaching babies to fight monsters. What kind of person does that?"\n\n"The kind who wants them to live," you answer.\n\nShe looks up. Her eyes are red. "Is that enough? Is wanting them to live enough to justify turning them into soldiers?"\n\nYou do not have an answer. The demon forest does not care about your moral questions. It only cares whether you survive the night.`,scene:'daily',mood:'#636e72',characters:['Emma','Norman','Ray'],effects:{stats:{cha:1,per:1}},choices:[{text:'Double down on training — survival comes first, childhood second. The children will understand when they are older.',next:'tpn_don_gilda_growth',effects:{stats:{str:1,per:1}}},{text:'Find a balance — train them, but also protect what childhood they have left. Stories at night. Games that are just games.',next:'tpn_don_gilda_growth',effects:{stats:{cha:1},relationships:{emma:2}}},{text:'This burden should not fall on Emma alone — step up as a co-leader and share the weight.',next:'tpn_don_gilda_growth',effects:{stats:{cou:1,cha:1}}}]};
TPN_NODES['tpn_don_gilda_growth'] = {text:`Don and Gilda change when you are not looking.\n\nDon was always brave — reckless, really. Loud where Ray was quiet, emotional where Norman was analytical. At Grace Field, he was the kid who punched walls when he was angry and cried openly when he was sad. Emma called him their heart. Ray called him a liability.\n\nIn the demon forest, Don becomes something else. The recklessness sharpens into decisiveness. He is the first to volunteer for dangerous scouts, the first to put himself between a threat and a younger child. He cannot strategize like Norman or invent like Ray, but he can act — instantly, without hesitation, with a physical courage that borders on supernatural.\n\nHe takes charge of the hunting parties. Not because anyone appointed him, but because the role needs filling and he fills it. He learns to track demon-world animals by scent and footprint. He teaches the other children to set snares, to gut and clean a kill, to build smokeless fires. His hands, which used to shake with unfocused energy, become steady.\n\n"I was useless at Grace Field," he tells you one night, cleaning a forged knife with mechanical precision. "Tests and scores and sitting still — that was never me. Out here, I know what I am good at. Out here, I matter."\n\nGilda's transformation is quieter but deeper. She was always the careful one — observant, methodical, anxious in a way that the farm system probably noted as a flaw. In the demon world, her anxiety becomes foresight. She sees problems before they happen.\n\nShe becomes the camp's organizer. Supply inventories. Rotation schedules. Medical logs for every child — temperature, hydration, morale, all tracked in a journal she maintains with religious discipline. When Norman is occupied with strategy and Emma is occupied with everything, Gilda keeps the machinery of survival running.\n\nBut her real gift is people. She notices when a child has not eaten. She notices when someone is crying silently after a demon encounter. She sits with them. She does not offer solutions — she offers presence. In a world of constant crisis, Gilda's calm attention is the closest thing to safety the younger children have.\n\nOne evening, you watch them — Don returning from a hunt with enough meat for three days, Gilda distributing it with a system that ensures the youngest eat first, the two of them working in wordless coordination.\n\n"They do not need us anymore," Ray says, watching the same scene. His voice is not sad. It is proud. "Not in the way they used to."\n\nEmma smiles. "Good. We were never supposed to carry this alone. We were supposed to raise people who could carry it with us."`,scene:'daily',mood:'#1a3a1a',characters:['Emma','Ray','Norman'],effects:{stats:{cha:1},relationships:{emma:1,ray:1}},choices:[{text:'Formalize the leadership structure — Don leads field operations, Gilda manages logistics, spreading the burden properly.',next:'tpn_leadership_weight',effects:{stats:{int:1,cha:1}}},{text:'Let it grow organically — forced structure could stifle what is developing naturally.',next:'tpn_leadership_weight',effects:{stats:{per:1},relationships:{emma:1}}}]};
TPN_NODES['tpn_leadership_weight'] = {text:`The first child you cannot save changes everything.\n\nHis name is Marcus. Eight years old. Quiet, clever, good with his hands — he helped Ray build the camp's water filtration system from nothing but charcoal and layered cloth. He was on a routine foraging run, three hundred meters from camp, in territory you had scouted and cleared twice.\n\nThe wild demon came from below. Burrowing species — something you had not encountered, something Mujika had not warned about, something that erupted from the forest floor like a landmine made of teeth. Marcus did not even have time to scream.\n\nDon reached him first. Don fought the demon with a ferocity that bordered on madness, driving the creature back with a torch and a forged spear. But the damage was done. Marcus bled out in Gilda's arms while she applied pressure to wounds that could not be pressured, whispering "Stay with me, stay with me" while his eyes went somewhere far away.\n\nThat night, the camp is silent. Not the disciplined silence of a well-trained unit. The silence of children who just learned that survival is not guaranteed, that doing everything right can still end in blood.\n\nEmma does not cry. She sits apart from the group, staring at Marcus's empty sleeping spot, and you can see her doing something terrible with her face — reconstructing the smile, piece by piece, because the children need her to smile. They need her to say it will be okay.\n\nDon hits a tree until his knuckles bleed. Gilda adds a name to a page in her journal that you did not know existed — a page of names. Marcus is not the first. He is the third.\n\nNorman calculates. He always calculates. "The burrowing species follows vibration patterns. We need elevated sleeping platforms. Movement in pairs minimum. Ground-contact time reduced by—"\n\n"Stop," Ray says. "Just... stop. For one night, stop calculating. A kid is dead."\n\nNorman stops. His hands are shaking.\n\nYou bury Marcus at dawn. Phil, who never cries, reads a passage from a book Marcus liked. The younger children place flowers. Emma says his name three times — once for who he was, once for who he would have been, once for the world that failed him.\n\nThen she turns to the group. Thirty-seven faces looking at her. Waiting.\n\n"We did not escape to be safe," she says. Her voice is raw. "We escaped to be free. Free means the world can hurt us. Free means we lose people. Free means every day is a choice to keep going when stopping would be easier."\n\nShe looks at each child, one by one. "I cannot promise you will all survive. I CANNOT promise that, and I will not lie to you the way Mama did. But I can promise you this: every one of you matters. Not as a score. Not as a product. As a person. And we will fight for each other — every single day — because that is what family does."\n\nPhil raises his hand. "Emma? Marcus mattered too. Right?"\n\n"Marcus mattered most of all, Phil. Because he is the reason we will never stop fighting."\n\nThe children rise. They pack the camp. They move forward.\n\nYou carry Marcus's toolkit — the pliers and wire he used to help build the water filter. You will give it to the next child who shows an aptitude for building things. Because that is what you do now. You carry the dead forward, and you give their gifts to the living.`,scene:'confrontation',mood:'#cc0000',characters:['Emma','Norman','Ray'],effects:{stats:{cou:1,cha:1},relationships:{emma:2}},choices:[{text:'Channel the grief into preparation — no more preventable deaths. Build better defenses, better protocols.',next:'tpn_minerva_shelter',effects:{stats:{int:1,per:1}}},{text:'The children need more than survival — they need hope. Push harder to find the Seven Walls and end this.',next:'tpn_seven_walls_labyrinth',effects:{stats:{cou:1},relationships:{emma:1}}}]};

TPN_NODES['tpn_ray_birthday'] = {text:`January 15th.

Ray does not mention it. He never mentions it — for eleven years, he has let the date pass without ceremony, accepting only the perfunctory birthday dinner Isabella arranges for all January children, smiling exactly the right amount.

Today, he cannot smile.

You find him at 3 AM by the wall. He is carrying a bottle of oil he stole from the shed and his expression is the expression of someone who has made a decision and is doing arithmetic to confirm it.

You take the oil from him.

He does not fight you. That is what frightens you most — he was expecting someone to stop him, the way you expect a door to open.

"It is the only variable that actually works," he says. His voice is a systems-analysis. There is no panic in it because the panic is what produced the plan. "If I cause a fire big enough, chaotic enough — if it takes a life, specifically mine, if Mama sees me consumed by it and stops looking for me — then you have a window. You, Emma, everyone. The trackers cannot transmit from ash. The manifest will show me as resolved. You get three, maybe four days of reduced surveillance while the investigation happens." He looks at the wall. "The math is clean."

"You planned this when you were six years old," you say.

"I planned it when I understood what we were." He turns. In his eyes is something that has been compressing for eleven years. "I thought I could be the piece that saves everyone else. I thought that was a good trade."

Emma appears from the dark. You have no idea how long she has been there. Her face is — there is no word for what her face is.

She takes Ray's hand. "No," she says. "I already accounted for you. You are in my plan too. You do not get to opt out of surviving."

She has her own bottle. Her own fire.

The fire she sets is real, and controlled, and exactly large enough to look like the ending Ray planned, and exactly small enough to be put out before it consumes anything irreplaceable.

Ray's eyes watch his own plan become something else. He almost looks angry. Then he almost looks like he might cry.

"You are an idiot," he tells Emma.

"Yes," she agrees. "But I am your idiot. Now come on. We have work to do."`,scene:'confrontation',mood:'#cc0000',characters:['Ray','Emma','Isabella'],effects:{stats:{cou:2,int:1},relationships:{ray:3,emma:2},flags:{ray_plan_countered:true}},choices:[{text:'Side with Emma — Ray lives, the plan evolves, and you begin the real escape preparation.',next:'tpn_escape_prep',effects:{stats:{cou:2},relationships:{ray:2,emma:2}}},{text:'Hear Ray out fully — his math is brutal but not wrong. There may be a version of his plan that keeps him alive.',next:'tpn_save_norman',effects:{stats:{int:2},relationships:{ray:2}}}]};


TPN_NODES['tpn_norman_alive'] = {text:`The message comes on a frequency only the three of you know.

Ray built the receiver from salvaged parts, the way he builds everything — quietly, over years, from pieces no one noticed were missing. The signal is weak and encrypted in a cipher constructed from their shared childhood: birthdays, bedtime story characters, Isabella's lullaby transposed into numbers.

Norman.

He is alive. He should not be alive. You watched the gate close behind him with Isabella's hand on his shoulder and you have been carrying the weight of that gate for months, through the forest, through Minerva's shelter, through every night Emma woke up crying and thought no one heard her.

He is alive. He is at a place called Lambda 7214. And the boy who sends this message is and is not the Norman you know.

His report is clinical. He describes Lambda the way you would describe a machine: inputs, outputs, purpose. The experiments. The other children there, the ones with exceptional scores who were pulled from farms across the demon world and placed in a facility designed to push human cognition past what the demons thought possible. The drug trials. The isolation protocols. The children who did not survive the process and the ones who did and what surviving cost them.

Norman survived. Norman, predictably, studied the place until he understood it better than its architects. Norman, terrifyingly, has a plan.

"The farm system is the target," he writes. "Not just escape. Elimination. The entire structure that makes farms possible — the demon clans that sustain it, the human collaborators that enable it, the Ratri clan that enforces it from above. I have the intelligence. I have the allies. I need you."

Emma reads over your shoulder. Ray reads over Emma's shoulder. No one speaks for a long time.

Then Emma says: "He sounds different."

"He sounds like someone who has been alone for too long with something terrible," Ray says.

"He sounds like me," you say, and realize as the words arrive that you mean it. The Norman who sends this message has been doing what you all did in Grace Field — enduring in order to act. Compressing. Waiting.

The coordinates are real. Lambda is real. Norman is waiting.`,scene:'revelation',mood:'#2a0a2e',characters:['Norman','Emma','Ray'],effects:{stats:{int:1,per:1},relationships:{norman:3},flags:{norman_alive:true}},choices:[{text:'Go to Lambda immediately — every day there is another day of whatever they are doing to him.',next:'tpn_lambda_experiments',effects:{stats:{cou:2},relationships:{norman:2,emma:1}}},{text:'Plan. Gather everyone. You are not walking into another facility without knowing what you are walking into.',next:'tpn_lambda_experiments',effects:{stats:{int:2},relationships:{ray:2}}}]};


TPN_NODES['tpn_lambda_experiments'] = {text:`Lambda 7214 is not a farm. It is a laboratory.

The distinction matters in the way that the distinction between a cage and a coffin matters: one implies the possibility of release.

Norman walks you through it quietly. He has that stillness now that comes from having processed horror through pure intellect until it became data. The children here — there are eleven of them, ranging from eight to fourteen — are not raised for harvest. They are raised for something the demon researchers call optimization. The discovery that human cognitive performance under controlled stress and chemical augmentation exceeds what demons considered physiologically possible changed the calculus of the farm system entirely.

The drugs are real and their effects are real. Pattern recognition that should be impossible becomes possible. Mathematical intuition that should take years develops in weeks. The body pays for it — shortened lifespans, systemic deterioration that the researchers log with interest but do not attempt to reverse.

You see the children. They look at you with the flat assessment of people who have learned that new faces are data points.

You see what Norman has built here, too. In the spaces between supervised periods, in the hours the researchers think are consumed by isolation protocols — Norman has been organizing. These children have been talking to each other in a language constructed from prior knowledge the researchers do not share. They have been building something.

"Trust is the most valuable resource in a closed system," Norman explains. He speaks about these children with the warmth he usually reserves for Emma and Ray, which means they have become his. "The researchers controlled for intelligence and underestimated community. That is the variable that ends this facility."

He looks at you. The Norman-shaped absence in your chest fills partially. Not completely. What is looking back at you has Norman's face and Norman's precision and something additional — a cold clarity purchased at considerable cost.

"I needed to see the whole system from inside it," he says. "Now I have. Are you ready to help me tear it down?"`,scene:'revelation',mood:'#2a0a2e',characters:['Norman','Ray','Emma'],effects:{stats:{int:2,per:1},relationships:{norman:2}},choices:[{text:'Yes — you are ready. Norman has the plan and you have people. It is enough.',next:'tpn_ratri_revelation',effects:{stats:{cou:2},relationships:{norman:2,emma:1}}},{text:'Ask about the cost. His plan tears down the system — but what does it cost the people inside it?',next:'tpn_ratri_revelation',effects:{stats:{int:1,cha:1},relationships:{norman:1}}}]};


TPN_NODES['tpn_emma_promise_price'] = {text:`The demon god does not look like a god.

That is the first thing you understand when you finally reach the place the old texts call the origin, the place where the promise between humans and demons was first made and where it can be unmade. The creature waiting there is vast and patient and does not have a face in any arrangement you can read. It is simply present, the way old things are present, occupying space with the confidence of something that has never needed to justify itself.

You came here because Norman's plan has a hole. The farm system can be dismantled. The Ratri clan can be negotiated with, eventually, given enough leverage. The individual farms can be freed. But the promise itself — the original agreement, the one that made humans into cattle in the first place — cannot be dissolved by any human action. It requires the god to release it.

The god is willing to release it. The god has a price.

Emma listens to the price. Her face does not change. Emma's face never changes when she has made a decision — it becomes very still, the way a lake is still when there is nothing disturbing its surface, and you have learned that the stillness means the depths are moving.

"All right," she says.

"Emma." Ray's voice has never sounded like that. "Emma, what are you agreeing to?"

"The memories." She turns to face you both. Her smile is the one she uses when she needs you not to see past it. "I forget. All of it. Everyone I know. It is not — it is not nothing. But compared to every child in every farm, in every world, getting to live?" She looks at her hands. The hands that climbed a wall and started a revolution. "That is not a hard trade."

You want to argue. You have the arguments ready — they are good arguments, they are correct arguments, Ray is already making them — but you look at Emma's face and you understand that she arrived at this answer not by calculation but by the same engine that has powered her since the moment she saw Conny in the truck. She does not weigh herself differently from other people. She has never learned to.

"I will remember for you," you say. You do not know if it helps.

"I know," she says. "That is why it is okay."

The god waits. Emma turns back to it. Somewhere above the demon world, the dawn is happening, or will happen, or has already happened — time works differently near original things.

She speaks the promise. Her voice does not shake.`,scene:'revelation',mood:'#1a3a1a',characters:['Emma','Ray','Norman'],effects:{stats:{cha:2,int:1},relationships:{emma:3,ray:2},flags:{emma_price_paid:true}},choices:[{text:'Honor the cost by making sure it was worth it — build the world Emma bought with her memories.',next:'tpn_ratri_captured',effects:{stats:{cha:2},relationships:{emma:2,ray:1}}},{text:'Find a way to give Emma her memories back — the deal was made, but deals can be renegotiated.',next:'tpn_human_world_arrival',effects:{stats:{int:2,cou:1},relationships:{emma:2,norman:1}}}]};


